#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t0-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t70
REASONING=The passage is a series of coordinated independent clauses describing Archias' relations (subject unexpressed throughout): **erat** (linking, with predicate *iucundus* and datives *Metello ... filio*), **audiebatur** (passive, agent *a M. Aemilio*), **vivebat** (intransitive, *cum Q. Catulo et patre et filio*), **colebatur** (passive, agent *a L. Crasso*), and **adficiebatur** (passive, *summo honore*), the last preceded by a *cum*-clause (**teneret**) and followed by a causal *quod*-clause (**colebant**), which itself contains a relative clause (**studebant**) and a conditional clause (**simulabant**). Notes on specific calls: - **devinctam** (t44): I take it as a predicate (circumstantial) participle with *teneret* ("held them bound by intimacy") rather than as a purely attributive modifier of *domum*, in line with the preference for the circumstantial reading; it agrees grammatically with *domum* (the nearest of the coordinated objects), so it points there via 'circumstantial participle'. An attributive reading ("the whole house of the Hortensii, bound by...") is defensible, hence the flag. - **qui** (t58): its antecedent is unexpressed — the relative clause as a whole serves as the subject of *colebant*. Rather than invent an antecedent token (the 'implied subject' type is reserved for participial antecedents), I give *qui* a 'subject' relation into *colebant* (the role the headless relative clause fills) and a second 'subject' relation into its own verb *studebant*, which takes 'unit verb' -> *qui*. An alternative would be to label the link to *colebant* 'relative pronoun'; I preferred the functional 'subject'. - **verum** (t64): taken as the adversative correlative of *non solum*, coordinating the two subject-expressions (*studebant* clause and the *si*-clause with *simulabant*). - **si qui** (t67): *qui* here is the indefinite (= *aliqui*), subject of *simulabant*, not a relative. - *percipere*/*audire* are complementary infinitives of *studebant*, so they anchor no verbal expression; *aliquid* is the object of *percipere*. - *simulabant* has no expressed object, so I classify it intransitive.

